5.2 Allotted times
DAY 1 FOR ALL ROOMS






10:00 am - 11:00 am: Checking in guests (raffle numbers are given here)


The competitors will arrive at the BRG eatery and will be checked in by two members of the organising team at the front desk, where they 
will be separated into the break rooms and assigned a raffle number; this raffle number will then be used to distribute the raffle prizes at the end of the day. While the front desk leaders check the students' IDs and record team attendance, four other Team Leads will act as wayfinders and guide the arriving groups directly to the break rooms that have been assigned to them so that they can unpack and get ready before the official welcome.


11:00 am - 11:30 am: Opening ceremonies


The YEC Chair and each of the Vice Chairs will make some opening remarks. The YEC Chair will start with a welcome speech in which he sets out the main objectives of the competition, after which the Vice Chairs will go on to look at the general safety guidelines, the competition rules, the venue regulations and the criteria for judging, followed by a short question and answer session before the students are sent to their rooms.


11:30 am - 2:30 pm: Phase 1 (Ice Breakers and Brainstorming)


During this period the competitors will have the opportunity to get to know one another, after which they will begin the first stage of the competition, this stage consisting of thinking and carrying out brainstorming on the engineering problem in question. The teams will start off with a short icebreaker activity lasting 15 minutes in order to define their team roles. They will then use the rest of the time to analyse the constraints, research the relevant background concepts, draw up preliminary designs and prepare their initial system architecture, while the Team Leads will keep an eye on the progress and provide answers to any rule queries.


2:30 pm - 4:00 pm lunch break (Domino’s catering)


Lunch will be provided, with Domino’s catering coming to serve it. The Catering Lead together with the two Team Leads will collect the delivery at the loading area by 2:15 pm and arrange the main dining area, putting in clearly marked sections for various diets such as vegetarian, halal, and gluten-free options. 


4:00 pm - 7:00 pm: Phase 2 (prototyping and designing)


Following lunch, the competitors will go to their assigned rooms and start the second stage of the competition, this stage involving the creation of a physical design or the production of a visual representation of their final project, according to the specifics of each competition. A central station for checking out tools will be opened under the guidance of 4 volunteers in order to safely hand out equipment such as hot glue guns, cutting mats, and testing tools. A 30-minute warning will be given by the organizers at 6:30 pm so that the teams can save their digital files and get their build areas in order.


7:00 pm - 8:00 pm: (Raffling and dinner)


The raffle will be held and the dinner will be provided by catering. Dinner will take place in the main hall from 7:00 pm to 7:30 pm, after which the live raffle draw will be carried out by the YEC Chair, with the winning numbers being announced and the sponsor prizes given out. The day will end with a short summary and some reminders about the overnight storage of the prototypes and room security before everyone is dismissed.


DAY 2 FOR ALL ROOMS 






10:00 am - 11:00 am: Checking in


Competitors will start arriving at the events area; they will be checked in by 2 members of the organising team at the front desk and separated into break rooms. Organizers at registration will log team attendance and escort participants back to their assigned work rooms, so teams can set up their workstations for the final sprint.


11:00 am - 2:00 pm: Phase 2 continuation


Competitors will continue to work on their 
project from the previous day. Teams will focus on finishing physical construction, running tests, resolving final bugs, and polishing their visual slidedecks. At 1:30 pm, a hard submission deadline will be enforced to collect all digital presentation files via USB or central upload link so room laptops can be pre-loaded.


2:00 pm - 3:30 pm: Lunch and potentially club workshop


While lunch is being served (Domino’s), Arbalest Rocketry will be hosting a workshop about a component used in the manufacturing of a rocket. Domino's pizza will be served from 2:00 pm to 2:45 pm, after which Arbalest Rocketry will lead an interactive technical presentation detailing specialized rocket manufacturing, structural components, and aerospace engineering techniques.


3:30 pm - 6:30 pm: Presentation and Judging


All the teams will have the chance to make a visual presentation about their project, after which we will proceed to judging. Each team will present sequentially to a panel of judges following a structured format of a 5-minute presentation, a 3-minute live prototype demonstration or test, and a 2-minute judge Q&A session, strictly timed by a designated Team Lead in each room.


6:30 pm - 7:00 pm: Closing remarks and Closure


The YEC team will be making some closing remarks after which we will wrap up the competition. While Vice Chairs aggregate judge scorecards to determine award winners, all teams will gather in the main auditorium for closing speeches by the YEC Chair and Vice Chairs.








6.0 Design Challenge 
6.1 Problem Statement


Engineers are frequently tasked with designing integrated systems that can precisely launch, deploy, and guide fragile payloads toward a specific target while protecting them from high-impact landings—whether delivering life-saving humanitarian supplies via targeted airdrop or safely deploying and landing an autonomous rover onto the surface of Mars.
6.2 Task


Design, build, and test a dual-component system, a launching mechanism and a lightweight protective lander to deliver a raw, large Grade A chicken egg to a designated target without breaking.
* Launch Parameters: The egg must be launched from a height of 29 inches.
* Target Accuracy: The lander must cover a distance of 80 inches to hit the center row and earn the maximum points.
* Landing Surface: The landing zone is a hard surface.
Points are awarded based on which zone of the 128-inch long by 32-inch wide rectangular target field the lander hits, measured from the Start line (0 inches):
* 72 inches to 88 inches
 (Center Target Zone, 16 inches long): 10 points (Maximum Points)
* 64 inches to 72 inches (Inner Left Zone, 8 inches long): 7 points
* 88 inches to 96 inches (Inner Right Zone, 8 inches long): 6 points
* 32 inches to 64 inches (Mid-Left Zone, 32 inches long): 5 points
* 96 inches to 128 inches (Far Right Zone, 32 inches long): 4 points
* 0 inches (Start) to 32 inches (First Zone, 32 inches long): 3 points
* Out of bounds (beyond the 128-inch length or outside the 32-inch width): 0 points
  

Numbers marked in blue are points, and numbers marked in red are dimensions (inches)
